/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-DERATING-SLIP-2026-09-10.md-38-## The argument that closes it
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-DERATING-SLIP-2026-09-10.md:39:Both halves are declared, so the honest counterargument is that their combination is the declared physics. Three things defeat that reading. The rate target is derived purely from a rate requirement with no margin term, and no sealed constant allocates a fade margin to provisioning — the 1.7 dB already folded into every threshold is a receiver implementation loss. The code's own comment calls the only headroom above the threshold "one negligible solver-scale step", explicitly a numerical device rather than a design margin. And the resulting service curve is non-monotone in a physically impossible direction: a beam with thirteen users, which the model declares infeasible, is forced to cap power, selects a high mode and **is credited**, while a beam with one user is credited nothing.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-DERATING-SLIP-2026-09-10.md-40-
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-DERATING-SLIP-2026-09-10.md-41-A model in which over-subscribing a beam thirteen-fold is the way to get it served is not describing radio propagation.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-DERATING-SLIP-2026-09-10.md-42-
