/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-FIGURE1-AND-BACKOFF-2026-09-09.md-38-**A user whose target mode is already the lowest mode has nothing to back off to, so its transmitted mode is `NO_MODE` by construction, for any reserve at all.** The first row of the table above shows this: at occupancy 1 the target is QPSK 1/4, which is also the PHY floor, and a reserve of even 1.0 dB credits zero bits.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-FIGURE1-AND-BACKOFF-2026-09-09.md-39-
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-FIGURE1-AND-BACKOFF-2026-09-09.md:40:This predicts an observation nobody had explained. The corrected engine reports `m_tx = NO_MODE` for about **62 %** of transmissions and `rate_target_feasible = false` for **40.8 %** of user-steps, while beam occupancy averages only 2.44. Low occupancy means a low target mode, a low target mode means no back-off room, and no back-off room means no transmitted mode. The high `NO_MODE` share is not primarily a link-budget accident; it is what the sealed back-off rule does at low occupancy.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-FIGURE1-AND-BACKOFF-2026-09-09.md-41-
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-FIGURE1-AND-BACKOFF-2026-09-09.md-42-A `NO_MODE` user still transmits at its computed power, still contributes interference, still burns PA energy and still sits in the availability denominator. That is the mechanism behind availability falling from 0.791 to 0.453.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-FIGURE1-AND-BACKOFF-2026-09-09.md-43-
--
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-FIGURE1-AND-BACKOFF-2026-09-09.md-51-
