/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-SUNI-OFFLINE-ARM-2026-09-10.md-2-Recorded 2026-09-10 from the offline certified-unilateral diagnostic. `DIAGNOSTIC_NOT_CLAIM`.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-SUNI-OFFLINE-ARM-2026-09-10.md-3-
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-SUNI-OFFLINE-ARM-2026-09-10.md-4-## The defect this closes
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-SUNI-OFFLINE-ARM-2026-09-10.md:5:Every previous run reported the unilateral comparator as identical to the neutral control, in both adjudications and again in the retraining pilot, where all five calls missed their five-second deadline after one iteration and executed the documented fallback. The cause was never the search: it was the discard-on-abort rule, which the sealed contract mandates as "atomic commit of the final profile only". Contract amendment v1.2 item 6 already requires a two-arm split — an offline arm for diagnosis, a deadline-bounded arm for operation. This is the offline arm, built and measured.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-SUNI-OFFLINE-ARM-2026-09-10.md-6-
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-SUNI-OFFLINE-ARM-2026-09-10.md-7-## It moves
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-SUNI-OFFLINE-ARM-2026-09-10.md-8-On 90 anchors of world 1, the offline arm selected a profile **different from its anchor at 73 (81.1 %)**. The other 17 were certified local optima at the anchor. So the arm is informative: the earlier degeneracy was an artefact of the budget, not evidence that the anchor is already unilaterally optimal.
--
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md-4-## The three answers that matter
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md-5-**Coverage is marginal, not simultaneous.** The calibration counts each of the three contrast intervals separately with denominator `3 * repetitions`. So `0.95^3 = 0.857` is **not** the benchmark and the measured **0.863 to 0.920 is genuine average marginal undercoverage** against 0.95. I doubted this at midday after an outside review raised the simultaneous-coverage possibility; the code settles it against me. My original reading was right.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md-6-
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md:7:A second, sharper point: the "95 % lower bound" is implemented as the **lower endpoint of an equal-tailed two-sided 95 % interval**, a 2.5 % lower-tail allowance, not a one-sided 95 % bound with a 5 % tail. The sealed wording does not distinguish these and they differ in both calibration and power.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md-8-
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md-9-**The 5 % date standard deviation is on the paired log contrast**, not on raw efficiency. The simulation adds the date effect directly to `log(1 + true gain)`, so it cannot cancel through pairing. That is the unfavourable of the two readings.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md-10-
--
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md-15-
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md-16-Worse, a two-way bootstrap over dates crossed with seeds has **nothing to resample on the date axis** when there is one date. Whatever interval width it reports on that axis is not estimated from data.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md-17-
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md:18:And the assumed 0.05 has never been measured: with one date the sample standard deviation of the paired contrast across dates is undefined. The only two-point comparison available is the same date at different checkpoints, `+0.376` at epoch 200 against `-0.291` at epoch 2000, which measures checkpoint sensitivity and says nothing about date variance.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md-19-
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md-20-Round 14's illustrative arithmetic, under its stated assumptions, put a true 2 % contribution at roughly **137 independent dates** and a 3 % contribution at roughly **50**. Against `D = 1` the gap is not a tuning problem.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md-21-
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md-22-## What this does and does not threaten
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md-23-It does **not** touch today's mechanism results. The interaction witness, the prevalence census at 30 of 30 anchors, and the perfect-knowledge ceiling of **+6.36 % over the certified unilateral optimum with the served count increased** are exact evaluations of a deterministic objective. They carry no bootstrap and no date clustering.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md-24-
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md:25:It threatens the **confirmatory claim**: the sealed intersection of three contrasts each clearing +0.5 % relative with a lower bound above it, plus three quality-of-service conditions. On one date that claim cannot be established at any seed count.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md-26-
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md-27-## The work this implies, and its character
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md-28-Generating more independent ephemeris dates is **world generation**, not training. It is independent of every learner defect found today and can proceed in parallel with all of them. It is also the item with the longest lead time, which is an argument for starting it early rather than after the learner is repaired.
--
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-DECISIONS-COUPLED-SOLVE-2026-09-08.md-2-
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-DECISIONS-COUPLED-SOLVE-2026-09-08.md-3-Trigger: the provider fix pass rehearsal on a real 100-user world failed closed with `ProbeError: invalid power certificate for BASE:nearest-eligible` (17.8 s wall, 937 MB RSS) before any arm was evaluated. An infeasible or slowly converging BASE is a legitimate physical state, not an error; the runner must never stop on it.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-DECISIONS-COUPLED-SOLVE-2026-09-08.md-4-
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-DECISIONS-COUPLED-SOLVE-2026-09-08.md:5:1. **Solver contract.** The capped rate-target map p ← min(cap, Γ·(N₀W + I(p))/ĥ) with `force_cap` rows is a standard interference function (monotone, scalable), so the iteration from p = 0 converges to the unique capped fixed point. Iteration budget raised from 4 096 to 65 536; convergence when the max absolute change ≤ 1e-10 W **or** the max relative change ≤ 1e-9. Convergence is checked on the update, not on a separate residual that treats cap-bound rows differently.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-DECISIONS-COUPLED-SOLVE-2026-09-08.md-6-2. **Certificates.** `CONVERGED` (tolerance met); `CONVERGED_SLOW` (budget reached, all changes over the last 1 000 iterations monotone and below 1e-6 W — recorded with the final change); `INVALID` only for non-finite values or a violated monotonicity check (a genuine implementation fault, which stops the unit). Cap-bound users are flagged `rate_target_infeasible`, transmit at cap, and are served iff their SINR after joint resolution ≥ SINR_min. A BASE with infeasible users is evaluated like any other profile.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-DECISIONS-COUPLED-SOLVE-2026-09-08.md-7-3. **KATs.** (a) a 3-user fixture with spectral radius 0.999 converges within budget to the analytic fixed point; (b) a fixture where two users' targets are jointly unattainable certifies `CONVERGED` with both at cap and `rate_target_infeasible`; (c) an injected NaN yields `INVALID` and stops; (d) the real-world rehearsal on the quarantined world 1 evaluates BASE:nearest-eligible without error and reports the certificate distribution over anchors.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-DECISIONS-COUPLED-SOLVE-2026-09-08.md-8-4. **Reporting.** Every receipt records the certificate per profile and the share of `CONVERGED_SLOW`; a high share is a physics finding to discuss, not a reason to change the model.
--
