## Part 2 — corpus and label proof

### 1. Label provenance

The deterministic samples use RNG domains
`EXACTTRAIN/20260910/{C1,C2,C3}`. C1 and C2 were sampled uniformly without
replacement from the 19,332 non-reference source rows; C3 was sampled from
all 4,552 multi-user rows. Excluding reference actions prevents the structural
C1 zero gauge from inflating the fallback match count.

| Route | Exact declared-target identities | Exact matches to sealed surrogate fallback | Match fraction |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| C1 | 2,000/2,000 | 0/2,000 | **0.0000%** |
| C2 | 2,000/2,000 | 359/2,000 | **17.9500%** |
| C3 | 2,000/2,000 | 0/2,000 | **0.0000%** |
| Combined stated sample | 6,000/6,000 | 359/6,000 | **5.9833%** |

For C1/C2, all 21,532 embedded surrogate comparison records were also checked
byte-for-byte against the corresponding sealed fallback rows. The corpus
training labels were checked against their exact rational extensions; maximum
absolute residuals were `5.3734794391857577e-14` for C1 and
`3.552713678800501e-15` for C2. C1 decomposition residuals were zero, and C2
was verified as forecast surplus minus persistence penalty. Thus the nonzero
C2 equality count consists of numerical coincidences with the fallback on
some rows; it is not a surrogate-label construction. The combined match rate
is far from one, while every sampled row passes its exact target identity.

